HOW TO RUN THEM:
  · `npm run check:fast` — six harnesses that would notice a broken build at all,
    in ~105s. This is the edit loop.
  · `npm run check` — the whole serial chain. Once, at the end, before a push.
    Never twice in a sitting, and NEVER two at the same time: every run begins by
    rewriting tools/game.html, so concurrent runs clobber each other mid-flight and
    neither result means anything.
  · There is no parallel mode. It was built, measured, and does not work yet —
    four harnesses go red at four-way that pass serially. See tools/README.md.

Two rules this file keeps learning the hard way, both worth reading first:
  1. A harness that passes on the broken build proves nothing. Several items
     below record a first assertion that did exactly that.
  2. Any new `ri()`/`rnd()` call at worldgen moves every body placed after it.
     Three separate harnesses have been broken by that one mechanism, and a fourth
     came close on the way in to this merge. THE FOURTH IS THE INSTRUCTIVE ONE,
     because the harness turned out to be the thing at fault, not the world.
     tools/rites.js came back "AT MAGIC 150 THE RITE IS OVER IN 39s" against a build
     whose RITE_WORK (120) and rate cap (2.0) were byte for byte the ones that had
     just measured 60s. THE ARITHMETIC IS THE TELL and it is worth copying: 120 work
     at a rate hard capped to 2.0 is sixty seconds and CANNOT be thirty-nine, so the
     rite was never completing early — it was ending the other way, through
     `collapseRite`. When a duration comes in under what the constants allow, stop
     reading the feature and go and find what ended it.
     What ended it: `riteTick` collapses the working if the ritualist finishes more
     than 2.5 tiles off the circle, and a crowd of summons arriving at the ring
     SHOVES them. The probe pinned the ritualist's STATE — it calls them "rooted and
     defenceless by design" — but not their TILE, so it was reporting a short rite
     for a body pushed out of its own ceremony. Pinning the tile fixes it, holds on
     both worldgen streams, and still fails four ways on the pre-fix build, which is
     the only thing that makes it worth keeping.

     AND A REAL LANDMINE, FOUND AND DELIBERATELY NOT DEFUSED. What flipped that
     lottery was one line: `seed('aether_cell', ... ri(3, 6) : ri(2, 4))` in the town
     stock, sitting directly above the garrison loop, so every guard and every body
     placed after a town with an occult dealer moves by one draw. That is exactly the
     rule above, freshly violated. It was fixed once — derived from the town's name
     instead of rolled, the same "derived, not rolled" pattern the sundered caches
     use — MEASURED, and then reverted, because removing the draw shifts the stream
     AGAIN and turned two other harnesses red:
       · guns.js `theLanceLandsInPlay` — the lance fires exactly ONCE in the probe's
         window, so the assertion is a single hit roll. It landed on two streams
         (100->88, 100->84) and missed on the third. A one-shot sample is the bug.
         NOW FIXED, AND IT WENT RED EXACTLY AS PREDICTED on a parallel branch —
         against a lance that works, one cell spent on both builds, which is the
         tell: the weapon fired, the arrow missed, and that was the whole assertion.
         WHAT MADE IT ONE SHOT was not the rate of fire (3.7s at atk 20, so twenty
         seconds is five shots) but the raider — it crosses the five tiles in about
         a second and a half, and a body inside 1.7 tiles makes the lance guard
         instead of shoot. The probe was measuring the opening shot of a fight and
         then a wrestle. It pins the target's TILE now, the same way rites.js had to
         pin the ritualist's, and widens to sixty seconds.
         AND THEN THE SECOND HALF, which the parallel branch did not hit because its
         lance still ran on cells: with the target held still the widened window
         fired FOURTEEN times and dealt nothing, because `foe()` hands out a tough-40
         body and the lance does 44 — the mark was face-down for thirteen of them. A
         soak at tough 400 gives eight shots and 43 blood, repeatable. It refuses to
         pass at all on fewer than four shots, so it cannot quietly decay back into a
         coin flip.
       · wanderers.js `opensOnArrival` — the walk arrives and the modal does not
         open. Note the failure text quotes a title that is still correct, because
         hiding a panel does not clear it; the display flag is the thing to read.
     A THIRD ONE OF THESE WAS FIXED RATHER THAN WRITTEN DOWN, because it went red on
     its own without any help: gunnery.js `aChargedLanceFires` watched a 6-SECOND
     window, which catches the lance mid-windup — two cells spent and a graze. It sat
     one rounding step from failing on every run (blood 99.6, printed as "100 to
     100", which is its own small lesson about rounding inside a pass message) and
     tipped over in one suite run out of three. At 30 seconds it is 100 -> 42 on 7
     cells, identical across runs, and the DRY case in the same loop is the control
     that keeps it honest: thirty seconds with no cells still does nothing at all.

     ONE OF THE TWO IS NOW PAID OFF, and the advice above held exactly: a bigger
     sample, no re-baselined number, a pinned target and a control in the same loop.
     `wanderers.js opensOnArrival` is the one still owed — it survived this change
     untouched, so it is still a live landmine and still wants the same treatment
     rather than a new number.

     So the draw stays for now and this is written down instead. Whoever removes it
     should expect to fix that assertion in the same sitting, and should fix it by
     giving it a bigger sample rather than by re-baselining a number.

