[DONE] The melee pathing system seems to have gone a bit janky. Everyone tries to beeline towards the enemy and gets caught on allies. They should attempt to flank around where possible. (Could be an unintended side effect of the “walling” effect we built to prevent enemies from beelining through allies to attack mages etc.)
    Your guess was close: not the walling effect, but the slide that was
    supposed to keep it from jamming. Proof in tools/press.js — 7 of its 11 assertions
    fail on the build before this one.
    MEASURED FIRST, because "janky" is not a number. Twelve bodies sent at one target
    from the same side, then counted by BEARING rather than by distance — six bodies
    standing 1.2 tiles away can be a neat ring or a heap on one side and only the
    bearings tell them apart. Old build: SEVEN of the twelve ended up in one eighth of
    the circle, three sides occupied out of eight, the tail trailing back to 4.5 tiles.
    New: two per side at worst, seven sides of eight, everybody inside 2.5.
    THREE CAUSES, and the first two are one line each.
      1. `separate()` had a slide that lets a blocked body go round rather than grind —
         and it asked `foes`, so it never fired for a body stuck behind its OWN side.
         An ally is the far more common thing to be stuck behind, because allies are
         the ones all walking at the same enemy.
      2. The same slide asked for `moveTarget || attackMove`. A body closing on
         `c.target` has NEITHER — the chase runs off `target` alone — so the one body
         most likely to be shoving through a crowd was the one excluded from going
         around it.
      3. THE REAL ONE. "Extras circle at spear-length and wait" is what the Kenshi cap
         comment says, and the code pushed the extra man straight back down the line he
         arrived on. So a queue stayed a queue and only got longer. Extras now walk
         round to their own arc and wait on it, which is what the comment always
         promised, and it is most of the measured difference.
    THE APPROACH ITSELF WENT THROUGH TWO ANSWERS and the second one is in the build.
    The first was a fan: each body took a stable place among everybody chasing the same
    target (by id — no dice, nothing to save) and swung its approach off the GROUP's
    mean bearing by that much. Off the group's, not its own: fanning off your own
    bearing is a feedback loop, because turning toward the offset moves the bearing the
    offset was measured from, and a body chases its own tail round the target — measured
    at three sides occupied, WORSE than changing nothing.
    A parallel branch answered the same report with a RING OF PLACES instead of an
    angle, and it is the better answer, so it is the one here. Each body claims one of
    eight slots around its quarry and walks to THAT. Nearest free place wins, which keeps
    the front rank walking straight in — the ones behind fan out because the near slots
    are already spoken for by the time they ask. A man already fighting somebody has a
    front and a back, so slots inside the arc he is facing cost an extra tile and a half
    of walking: a squad joining a fight in progress goes round to his flanks rather than
    queueing in front of his blade. Held half a second at a time and stored as an OFFSET,
    so it follows a quarry who is moving rather than aiming where he was.
    Two things were added to that branch's version. A slot is DEAR when occupied, not
    SHUT: rejecting a taken slot outright leaves four of twelve bodies with no slot at
    all, and a body with no slot walks at his tile, which is the beeline this replaces —
    measured on that branch at four sides covered with six stacked on one. Pricing
    occupancy above the width of the whole ring means a free place always beats a shared
    one and the overflow doubles up on the emptiest side. And the Kenshi extras now wait
    on the bearing of their OWN slot, a stride outside the fighting ring, so the men who
    cannot get hold of it are already spread around it rather than queued behind it.
    Twelve onto one: six sides of eight, no more than four on any of them, nobody
    travelling less than 7.9 tiles. Eight onto one: seven sectors, none of them stalled.
    Three joining a fight already in progress: 3 of 3 come round off his face.

[DONE] Kiting should slow down the ranged attacker. It doesn’t make sense that someone could run backwards at full speed while still firing projectiles. And catching a ranged attacker in melee should somewhat lock them down from fleeing again. (Somewhat — they should reasonably be able to escape if, say, an ally also engages that enemy.)
    Proof in tools/kiting.js, which measures GROUND COVERED and never a flag —
    the complaint is written in ground, and every flag read correctly on the broken
    build. The note below was right that the AI never kites, and that turned out to be
    the useful half: NOTHING in this game fires on the move. The AI branch closes OR
    shoots, and a player move order runs through `clearOrders`, which drops the target.
    So there was no "firing while running" to slow down.
    NOR WAS THE DRAW THE HOLE, which is where a plausible fix went and came back out.
    `physics` already plants the feet for the whole length of a windup, and a probe that
    gives the run order on the tick the draw opens measures 0.00 tiles walked before the
    arrow leaves. The answer is arithmetic: a bow at atk 10 is a 2.3s cycle of which the
    rooted draw is 0.45, so a perfectly rooted archer still walks four fifths of it flat
    out — measured at 86% of the ground the same archer covers doing nothing but running.
    THE RELOAD IS THE ONLY PART OF THE CYCLE BIG ENOUGH TO PAY FOR THE SHOT. `reloadT`
    runs from the loose until the weapon is ready again (the same window `atkCd` runs),
    and holds the legs at a fraction graded per weapon — a bow is nocked half-walking at
    0.62, a crossbow is spanned against the body at 0.50, the lance has to spin something
    up at 0.42. An archer who keeps shooting never gets their legs back; one who stops
    has them in a couple of seconds. Twelve seconds of hard kiting covers 68% of the
    ground the same archer covers just running, against 86% before.
    THE LOCK-DOWN, and the escape clause is the better half. `pinGrip(c, o)` is a scale,
    not a switch: alone with you the man holding you has both hands on you (0.50), one
    other body on him and most of the grip is gone (0.85), two and it is a formality
    (0.95); a second blade on YOU tightens it again. So the way out is not a timer or a
    dice roll, it is that a friend came and gave the man holding you something to think
    about, which makes fetching a caught archer a thing you can do rather than a stat.
    IT IS DIRECTIONAL, enforced in `stepToward` and not in `moveSpeed`: closing, circling
    at spear-length and standing your ground all cost nothing. Only leaving costs.
    AND A GRIP IS RELEASED, NOT OUTRUN — the part that took measuring. Contact is a
    1.45-tile test, and a body that steps out of it runs flat out on the very next tick
    while the man holding it is mid-swing with his feet planted: the gap opens to 2.3
    tiles in three ticks and an equal-footed chaser never closes it again. Scoped to
    contact alone the whole grip was worth 0.7 tiles out of thirteen. So it rides for
    0.9s past the last contact on two conditions that both mean he still has you — he is
    still coming for YOU, and he is still within arm and a stride. Four seconds of
    running: 13.4 tiles alone, 7.3 with a swordsman on them, 13.0 once an ally has that
    swordsman by the throat.
    EVERY NUMBER FALLS OUT OF `separate()`, which is the only pass in the tick that
    already walks every close pair — and it has to be there rather than in a scan of its
    own, because `charsNear` hands back ONE SHARED BUFFER: a scan issued from inside
    `moveSpeed` would empty the caller's list, and a scan nested inside another empties
    its own. (A parallel branch answered this item with a nested one. Same rule, quieter
    failure.) One tick of lag on the result, deliberately: it is a grapple, not a trigger.
    CONTACT IS COUNTED BEFORE THE SEPARATION CULL. Bodies are pushed apart to 0.8 and a
    blade reaches 1.45, so counting after the cull would have meant nobody ever held
    anybody. A bow cannot hold you, and neither can a mule or anything `noFight`; and
    nothing over 1.6 big is held by one man on its flank.
    AND THE LOOSE THREAD IS CLOSED TOO, in the commit after this one — a body holding a
    ranged weapon inside 1.7 tiles now falls through to the swing instead of standing
    there. It was found rather than remembered: guns.js went red after the changes above
    shifted the timing, and the diagnostic read `d=0.92`, eighteen cells in the stash, an
    enemy on its chest and eighty-five seconds of unused cooldown. Filed twice as "the
    same branch", and it was.
[DONE] Skeleton versions of Chimeras would be nice to have so that they don’t take the default human skeleton after being raised up. (Especially for lieutenants.)
    Built as part of the raise-mechanic rework below; the two were one job.
    The cause was not `kin` at all — it is that the chimera geometry lives in TWO
    blocks in buildCharMesh and both are gated `&& !c.undead`: the scaleborn block
    (~18502) and the general line block (~18577). So the lines were not colliding in
    the cache, they were simply never built for a dead body. There is now a third
    block, `if(risen && c.race === 'chimera')`, carrying a bone version of each of
    the four lines — houndkin's muzzle and tail, the ox's horns and shoulder blades,
    the thinblood's stretched skull, the scaleborn's crest, dorsal spines, bare wing
    spars and tail. Bone only: no fur, no scales, no membrane, no veins.
    Lieutenants get it for free — that branch already copied `race` and `sub`.

