[DONE] Add a new race: Mimics. A catch-all term for the mysterious beings who live alongside
       humans and have learned to be one with them. The imperfect mirror to the Messengers.
       Succubus (female only, charm, fertile with sterile races); Doppelganger (change form —
       fresh skin, loses bounty and relationships); Fallen (male only, no alchemy by choice,
       charismatic, permadeath in a burst of Ainzopha'arian light). Unique models for all.
    Proof in tools/mimics.js — twenty assertions, and the models read off the built MESH
    rather than off a flag, which is the rule the helms taught this repo.
    THE RACE. `mimic` in RACES with three lines in SUBRACES. The character creator needed
    nothing: it lists `Object.keys(RACES)` and `subsFor(race)`, so the race and its lines
    appear in the picker the moment the tables have them.
    TWO LINES ARE ONE SEX, and the rule holds against the CALLER: `sexOnly` on the line beats
    an explicit `sex` passed to `makeChar`, because a male succubus is not a preference, it is
    a body that does not exist. The harness asks for the wrong one on purpose.
    AN ART YOU ARE, NOT ONE YOU LEARNED. Every formula in this game is gated on a gift, an
    attunement, a magic minimum and often a piece of research — right for an art you study,
    wrong for one the body simply does. `innateArt` skips all four and keeps the two that make
    it a decision: mana and cooldown. The succubus is born with the Loyalty of Dust; a human
    beside her with more magic and the same mana still cannot cast it.
    THE STERILE CLAUSE is on the PAIR and not on her. `fertile` was read straight off the race
    table, which is correct for every line that existed when it was written. `pairBreeds` asks
    `traitOf` so a line can overrule its race, and then makes the succubus an exception to the
    other side as well — that asymmetry is the whole claim, and it is measured against a golem.
    CHANGING FORM CLEARS WHAT A FACE OWNS AND NOTHING ELSE. Every warrant in the world is void,
    because a warrant is out for a face. The marriage goes too, and the bonds — that is what it
    costs. What does NOT go is the town's REPUTATION: how a place feels about the necromancer
    walking its streets is about the necromancer, and no amount of new nose fixes it. Keeping
    those apart is what stops this being a delete-consequences button, and the harness pins it.
    PERMADEATH IS AN ABSENCE. There is no "cannot be raised" flag in this game and none was
    added: a Fallen is not pushed to `corpses`, so there is no body — nothing to raise, harvest,
    loot or bury — enforced by there being nothing rather than by a check in four places
    somebody has to remember. A human killed in the same breath is the control.
    CHARISMA IS THE MIRROR OF A MECHANISM THAT EXISTED. A scaleborn at a counter puts prices up
    15%; a Fallen puts them down 10%, by the same trick in the same line of code. Forgiveness
    reads MINOR off the town's own bounty table rather than a second list, so it moves if the
    table ever does, and it is a roll — "occasional", and a charm that always works is a permit.
    THE MODELS. Succubus: swept horns, a five-segment tail with a spade, folded wings on
    rather less than half of them — and a FACE, which took three passes and is the part worth
    writing down. The generic face is four boxes and deliberately neutral: a hard shadow bar
    across the eye line, two eyes, a brow, a mouth. That bar earns its place — it is what tells
    you which way a townsman is facing at thirty tiles — but on her it reads as a VISOR, and a
    woman in a visor is not the note. So hers is drawn ON TOP of the generic face rather than
    instead of it, each layer a fraction further out (band .155, white .1585, iris .1615, light
    .1645, lash .1665), because anything level with what it covers z-fights instead.
      · The first attempt put a thick lash bar above the eye with a gap under it, and a dark
        rectangle over each eye with a lighter strip between reads as SPECTACLES. Thinned and
        dropped onto the lid, so the eye and its lash are one shape.
      · The band is lifted the same way the named faces already lift theirs, and a real eye
        built on it: white, a coloured iris (four to choose from, off the id), and ONE BRIGHT
        PIXEL, which is the whole difference between a painted face and one looking at you.
      · The smile is geometry and not a colour — a fuller lip with a highlight along the top
        and the corners rotated up. A mouth that is one flat bar reads as a line whatever
        colour you make it.
      · And she always wears her hair long. The ordinary builder gives a woman long hair about
        four times in five; that is right for a crowd and wrong for the one line whose
        silhouette is meant to catch the eye. Filled in only when the generic pass would have
        skipped it, in the colour it would have used, so it never doubles up.
    The harness measures her against a plain WOMAN rather than the generic human, because `fem`
    already buys the narrower shoulder and wider hip that every woman in the game has — what is
    left over is the face, and it is 31 boxes of it.
    AND THEN A FIGURE AND A PALETTE, asked for after the face landed.
    THE FIGURE IS THE FRAME, NOT BOXES ON TOP OF IT. `build` on the line multiplies the same
    constants `fem` already sets — shoulder, pelvis, chest, limb, height — so the arms, legs,
    torso AND the garment all read it. The first attempt stated the waist and hip as three
    skin-coloured slabs in the line block instead, and they came out wider than the clothing
    they were meant to sit under: four women in barrels. The body is a stack of layers and a
    shape belongs at the bottom of it.
    A LINE MAY NAME A PALETTE. `skin` gives every body in a line one hide, which is right for a
    rock golem and wrong for anything meant to read as a person — four succubi together were
    four identical pink women. `skins` is picked per body off the same hash as the rest of the
    colouring: rose, ash, soot, violet, dusk and ember, none of them a colour a person comes in.
    AND EVERY MARK ON HER HAS TO SURVIVE HER OWN HIDE, which is what the palette cost. Every
    detail was derived by DARKENING the skin — horns, lash, lip, tail spade — and on the soot
    one that is invisible. The luminance is read once and the derivation flips: pale horns, a
    pale lash, a lip lifted instead of deepened. Same shapes, opposite direction.
    The harness asks the RESOLVED colour the model was built from rather than the table, because
    a palette nothing reads is a palette that changes nothing on screen: ten of them come up in
    five different hides. Doppelganger: no ornament at all — a face left smooth where
    a face has features, seams at the collar, waist and elbows, and one shoulder a little higher
    than the other. A doppelganger that stands out has failed at the one thing it does.
    Fallen: `oldGodPlate` — carved musculature no living body has, a mantle, a laurel nobody
    gave it, and glowing eyes, which are the part it did not choose.
    AND THE MESSENGERS GOT A MODEL OUT OF IT. They never had one and came out wearing the
    generic hunched gaunt rig, which is the wrong shape for the one Watcher that stands upright
    and talks to you. They wear `oldGodPlate` too, on a taller frame: the Fallen is one of these
    that walked out and made itself smaller, and that is the only difference the player needs to
    see. The carving sits PROUD of the garment on purpose — the first version put it at z 0.02,
    inside the shirt box, where it was perfectly invisible.
    NOTHING IN EITHER MODEL IS ROLLED. `buildCharMesh` runs from `syncChars` at frame rate, so
    a `rnd()` there is the renderer holding the simulation's dice — the bug this session already
    fixed once. Every variation is `hash2` off the body's id, which is also stable: a mimic that
    walks out of view and back is the same mimic.
    WORLDGEN IS UNTOUCHED, checked and not assumed — identical body set, order and first-600
    hash. Mimics are NOT in `rollNpcRace` yet and that is deliberate: adding them there changes
    which race a roll produces, and a race WITH subraces where the old answer had none calls
