[DONE] Magic skill grows fairly quickly. I think this is an old call of mine that simply having undead followers should train magic, but I'm realizing this makes it far too easy to train the skill without really doing anything.
    Found where the note said it would be — a passive tick in the main loop:

        if(c.gift==='dark'){ const n = risenCount(c);
          if(n > 0) xpGain(c, 'magic', dh * 0.012 * Math.min(n, 8)); }

    `dh` is game-hours, so a full host paid about 2.3 magic A GAME DAY for
    existing. Measured: eight days of a host standing in the yard, nobody
    moving and nothing dying, was MAG +9.24. A month of that is seventy points
    of the hardest skill in the school, earned by leaving the game running.
    NOT DELETED — MOVED, because the note's own warning is the real constraint:
    a caster who never trains by casting is the opposite failure. The same idea
    and the same words ("commanding the dead sharpens the craft") now fire at
    the moment the host does the thing a host is for. A kill taken by one of
    your risen sharpens its binder. So it costs bodies and risk instead of
    patience: a campaigning necromancer earns about what the old idle tick paid,
    and one who raises a wall of skeletons and goes to bed earns nothing.
    Deliberately no distance test — a band sent out under a commander is still
    your working, and gating on proximity would punish the one system built for
    sending them away. And it is the BINDING that learns: a risen nobody is
    holding teaches nobody.
    After: idle 8 days MAG +0.00, a host taking six kills a day MAG +16.80, and
