    — and leaves an OWED SHIFT on the body. Reach the post in daylight and it is
    spent there with a float over the hands that did it; never get there and the
    next rollover spends it silently, which is what kept the shelves stocked
    before any of this existed. A town you have not visited in forty days must
    not be a town that has not eaten in forty days.
    THREE MEASUREMENTS ON THE WAY, ALL WORTH KEEPING:
      · EIGHT SMITHS CANNOT STAND IN ONE DOORWAY. Copperhold has eight and one
        forge. Pointing all eight at the same tile put eight bodies inside each
        other and `separate` shoved them back out past the radius that counts as
        being at work — arrive, be pushed off, walk back, forever. Each now has
        its own square metre of the yard, drawn off its id so it is the SAME
        square metre every morning: a bench they are shuffled around at random is
        a queue, not a bench.
      · THE PITHEAD, NOT THE FAR END OF THE SEAM. The first version sent every
        miner to the CENTRE of the ore field, thirty to fifty tiles outside the
        wall. Measured: 12, 31, 37 and 44 tiles short at three in the afternoon,
        having walked cross-country through a gate all day. A town does not empty
        half its population into the wilderness every morning. The head sits a
        few tiles past the fences on the town's own bearing to the vein, where
        you can see them from the rampart.
      · AND `near` IS PER-POST. Two and a half tiles is right for a doorway and
        absurd for a pit.
    THE THROUGHPUT INVARIANT IS ASSERTED EXACTLY AND NOT STATISTICALLY. Counting
    SHELVES only says it loosely — two builds run different PRNG streams, and
    eight days of plague, war and death move a town's output by a third on their
    own (232 units on one build, 163 on the other, both perfectly correct).
    Counting SHIFTS against worker-days is exact: 0.62 a hand a day against the
    0.70 the productive roll has always paid out.
    AND NOBODY DRAWS FOR AN EMPTY STREET. `showWork` runs on every unit of every
    town's economy; a cheap camera box first, then real visibility, so seven
    towns nobody is looking at cost one subtraction each. Asserted: forty units
    made two hundred tiles away draw exactly nothing.
    AND ONE PROBE FAULT, which is the same one twice. `HOUR_SEC` is 8, so 240
    real seconds is THIRTY game-hours: the first draft ran the town past dusk and
    through the next midnight and then asked why nobody was at work.

[DONE] New giant enemy: Wyrms. Imperfect dragons with both ranged and sweeping
melee attacks. Quite rare to encounter but powerful and known to hoard vast
amounts of gold.
    Proof in tools/wyrm.js. 3 red on the build before, 0 after.
    IMPERFECT IS THE WORD THE WHOLE CREATURE IS BUILT OUT OF. The Scaleborn are
    dragon-blooded and everybody knows it; a wyrm is what the same work looks
    like when it did not come out right. The wings are there and they do not
    carry it — folded hard against the ribs and plainly too small, which is the
    one piece of geometry that has to say `imperfect` on its own.
    THE SWEEP HAD TO BE BUILT, NOT CONFIGURED, and this is the part worth
    keeping. The cleave that catches bystanders is gated `if(wpn && !wpn.range)`
    — it comes off the WEAPON. So everything in the game that fights with what it
    IS (`clawDmg`) struck exactly one body per blow however large it was, and six
    men could stand shoulder to shoulder in front of a house-sized animal and
    take it in turns. `sweep` is that idea for things with nothing in their
    hands, reading the same three numbers a MOVE does — arc, reach, and how much
    of the blow the bystanders take. Nothing else in the world has one, and the
    harness asserts that too: `sweepAfter` runs on every landed blow of every
    body carrying the record, so if anything else picked one up the whole melee
    changed and this is the only file that would say so.
    MEASURED BY DIFFERENCE. Twelve blows aimed at one man in a rank of five
    reach 5 of them with the sweep and 1 without. An absolute number would pass
    on any build where the thing merely hits hard, which is precisely the failure
    this is about: a creature that struck one man per swing and hit like a house
    would look completely fine.
    THE BREATH IS A LINE, NOT A DISC. Nine tiles down a bearing, widening the
    way a breath does, and it will not be used on something already in its teeth
    — that is what the teeth are for. Stepping out of the bearing is the answer
    to it, which a disc would not allow. Asserted with a rank in the line and two
    bodies outside it.
    RARE MEANS FAR, NOT JUST FEW. Two in the world, sixty tiles from any town and
    a hundred and ten from where you start. A wyrm you meet on the road on day
    three is a wall, not an encounter. Measured: the nearest is 386 tiles out.
    AND THE HOARD IS MEASURED AGAINST THE RICHEST THING ALREADY HERE rather than
    against a number somebody liked the look of — 12,447 against the Red
    Baroness's 2,102, which is 5.9x. A hoard worth less than the trip is a purse.
    THREE PROBE FAULTS, ALL WORTH WRITING DOWN:
      · `w.neutral = false; w.provoked = true` takes a body OUT of the rule that
        was going to make it hostile. `hostile()` reads the two as a pair — "a
        neutral that has been started holds the grudge against the player" — so
        clearing the flag leaves a fauna hostile to nobody at all. The probe
        reported a breath that caught nothing, about a breath that works.
      · `syncChars` builds rigs for what is on screen and nothing else. Staging
        the rig probes at a fixed 600,600 while the camera sat at 1089,554
        reported "THE RIG DID NOT BUILD" about three rigs nobody had asked for.
      · The size bound passed at 11.75 against a ceiling of 12 — a 2% margin,
        which is the mimics.js lesson from three commits ago wearing a new hat.
        The creature was trimmed until the bound is a real guard rather than a
        hair's breadth.
    AND `big` IS NOT DECORATION. It feeds reach, pick radius and walking speed as
    well as scale. At 2.6 the first draft came out thirteen and a half tiles nose
    to tail, longer than most buildings in the game. It sits at 2.2 — the same
    scale as the Grazer — so its bulk comes out of its PROPORTIONS: 732 triangles
    against the Grazer's 168, and long and low (2.91) where the Grazer is a
    barrel (1.50).

[DONE] Subraces to cut: Thinblood (pointless and redundant) and Cellar-line
