[LOOKED] "Pathfinding is wonky now. Sometimes allies start rotating around each
other in a glitchy spiral circle, and never reach their destination."
    I COULD NOT REPRODUCE IT, and I am saying so rather than claiming a fix.
    Three stagings in tools/spiral.js, all of them the shapes that should trigger
    it: four crossing open ground shoulder to shoulder, five sent to the SAME
    tile (which is what clicking the ground does), two swapping places, and three
    closing on one enemy — the last because a couple needs bodies to STAY inside
    the separation radius to wind up, and a chase does that indefinitely. Every
    order completed in every case, on the build before my change and after.
    WHAT I DID FIND, by reading rather than by measuring, is a real defect of the
    same shape: the sidestep in `separate` — "a body with somewhere to be goes
    round, not through" — picked a FIXED perpendicular, pushing `a` along
    (-uy, ux) and `b` along exactly the opposite. That is a couple, and a couple
    is a rotation; it never asked the one question that makes it a slide rather
    than a spin, which is WHICH WAY IS THE GOAL. It takes the perpendicular that
    makes progress now, so two bodies walking the same way step the same way
    instead of winding each other up, and two walking into each other still pick
    opposite sides and pass. Measured improvement on the open-field case: the
    worst body's travel around the group fell from 0.41 turns to 0.24.
    AND I THREW ONE FIX AWAY. I also had a rule that completed a move order when
    a body could no longer get closer — reasonable-sounding, and the A/B showed
    orders already complete on both builds, so it fixed nothing observable while
    changing move-order semantics globally. Unverified global change addressing a
    symptom I could not reproduce: reverted.
    MY OWN FIRST METRIC WAS THE WORST OF IT. Accumulating each body's angle about
    the group's centroid reported a SETTLED HEAP as 4.56 "turns", because a body
    sitting a tenth of a tile from the centre swings through large angles on
    sub-tile jitter — the metric exploded exactly where the bodies were most
    still, and I nearly "fixed" the game on the strength of it. Path length over
    net displacement has no such singularity: a straight walk is about 1, a
    circler climbs without bound, a body standing still contributes nothing.
    STILL NEEDS: what were they doing when it happened — under orders, in a
    fight, following, on a patrol job, underground? The harness is registered and
    will hold whatever shape turns out to be the real one.

[DONE] Raising Old Bones massively speeds up the "notice" dial. It should not,
as they are weak undead and not a genuine threat.
    Proof in tools/hosts.js. The A/B is about as blunt as one gets: before the
    fix, EIGHT BONE SOLDIERS COST EXACTLY WHAT EIGHT BOUND RISEN COST — host
    weight 8 against 8, noise 0.0200 a tick against 0.0200. Indistinguishable
    from a standing army, on both dials, which is the whole of the report.
    TWO DIALS, TWO SEPARATE FAULTS.
    `hostSize` is the `bindWeight || 1` trap that `risenLoad` already carries a
    note about, in a second place nobody checked when the first was fixed — Old
    Bones and Mass Reanimation both set `bindWeight = 0`, and `0 || 1` is 1, so a
    body that weighs nothing weighed a full one. It feeds the standing notice
    bump AND the menace flag, so four bone soldiers were also making the Order
    treat you as a necromancer with a host.
    `hostNoiseTick` defaults anything undead to 0.75 noise apiece. A propped body
    is 0.15 now — they still make SOME noise, because the raising itself was
    heard, but eight of them no longer clear the threshold the dial starts at.
    Now: weight 0, noise 0.0000 against a bound host's 0.0104.
    AND THE PROBE TOOK THREE GOES, all three of them my own fault and all three
    the same fault — measuring the wrong thing.
    · First it ran the world ten seconds with each host and diffed `noticed`,
      which came out NEGATIVE, because `update` also DECAYS the dial and advances
      a world with other reasons to move it.
    · Then it read `hostSize` after ONE tick and got 0 for a host of eight, because
      the recompute is on a one-second sub-tick and a single frame never reaches it.
    · The version that works measures the two INPUTS, which are deterministic and
      are exactly what changed. The needle is only f(inputs).

[OPEN] Succubi hair is off — the bangs near the forehead jut out awkwardly, and
some of them are bald on top after getting recruited.
    REVERTED. The fix below was rejected on sight — "the succubi look terrible
    now. You can see their forehead and they still have that odd band of hair
    over their face" — and the geometry is back exactly as it was (verified by
    diffing the file against the commit before it: no difference at all).
    WHAT THE NEXT ATTEMPT NEEDS TO KNOW. The wrapped shell fixed the bald crown
    and stopped the strands projecting, and made the head WORSE, so the fault was
    never really the depth numbers:
      · the fringe still reads as a horizontal BAND across the face rather than as
        hair. Four strands of different lengths side by side still line up into a
        bar at this resolution, because they all start at the same y and the eye
        reads the top edge, not the ragged bottom one.
      · and there is bare forehead between the crown and that band — a lit strip
        of skin, which is what makes it read as a headband rather than a fringe.
        Widening the crown did not close it because the gap is vertical, not in z.
    So the two candidates are: bring the fringe UP to meet the crown so there is
    no skin between them and let it hang to different depths, or take the second
    option originally offered and remove the fringe entirely — a plain shell to
    the brow line with the eyes doing all the work. The measurements below are
    still true and still useful; the conclusion drawn from them was not.
    ORIGINAL DIAGNOSIS, KEPT:
    BOTH HALVES WERE THE SAME BLOCK, which is why they were reported together.
    The crown was `B(0.30, 0.105, 0.15, hairc, 0, 0.345, 0.092)` — 0.15 deep,
