    THE FRONT DOOR. What was there appended a bare CONTINUE button to the BOTTOM
    of the start overlay when a save existed — under the whole creator, which is
    where everything else had ended up too. Two doors now, and the creator is
    behind one of them. CONTINUE is only offered when there is something to
    continue; with no save the screen says so rather than showing a button that
    leads nowhere, and a corrupted save says THAT rather than failing silently.
    THE WAY BACK OUT IS A RELOAD, and the reason is worth writing down rather
    than looking like laziness: the world lives in module-level state — `chars`,
    `decks`, `blocked`, `baseBlocked`, every worldgen array — built once as the
    script executes. `restore` can put a saved world back on top of that, but
    there is NO CODE PATH THAT UNBUILDS ONE, and inventing one to serve a menu
    button is a large new surface for exactly the class of bug this file already
    has a dozen notes about. So it saves, then reloads into the front door, where
    CONTINUE is waiting with the run just saved. The save is awaited and its
    failure is reported — dropping somebody back to the menu having quietly lost
    the afternoon is the one outcome that must not happen.
    `storeSave()` is the browser half of a save on its own, because `btn-save`
    also downloads a file and nobody wants a download every time they back out.
    TEN THOUSAND GOLD sits next to REVEAL MAP, which is the other honest cheat in
    that menu, and is labelled "playtesting only" the same way.
    DRIVEN END TO END rather than eyeballed: no save → only NEW GAME; NEW GAME →
    step one; `btn-start` from there still boots (771 bodies); the gold button
    2000 → 12000; save & quit writes 106,897 characters; reload → CONTINUE
    appears; CONTINUE restores the run WITH THE 12,000 STILL ON IT, which is what
    proves it is the same run and not a fresh one.
    AND start.js CAUGHT IT AGAIN, in a way worth recording: the creator's nav
    rail is hidden behind the front door, and A RAIL WHOSE PARENT IS
    `display:none` STILL REPORTS ITS OWN COMPUTED DISPLAY — so the probe read a
    live button at 0x0 in the corner and tapped the wallpaper. It opens the door
    with a finger now, like a player, and counts it: five taps from the front
